---
import Prose from "../layouts/Prose.astro";

const title = "The original Jostraca";
const description =
  "The history of the original Java Jostraca code generator, its releases, " +
  "design, contributors, and relationship to the current project.";

const ARCHIVE =
  "https://web.archive.org/web/20130901123451/http://www.jostraca.org/";
---

<Prose
  title={title}
  description={description}
  lede="Before the TypeScript and Go implementations, Jostraca was a Java toolkit for template-driven code generation. This is the project the name and ostracon came from."
>
  <figure class="legacy-mark">
    <img
      src="/brand/ostracon-restored.png"
      width="1104"
      height="1424"
      alt="The original Jostraca ostracon: a drawn profile under the handwritten project name"
    />
    <figcaption>
      The original project mark, restored from the surviving 74 × 100 pixel web image.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="project">The Java project</h2>

  <p>
    The original Jostraca described itself as a general-purpose code generation
    toolkit for software developers. It treated generation as “programming by
    example”: give the computer a representative file and let a template produce
    the repeated variations. The generator was written in Java and used Java
    Server Pages syntax, but the files it produced did not have to be Java.
  </p>

  <p>
    Its supplied configurations covered Java, JavaScript, Perl, Python, Jython,
    Ruby, Rebol and C. A Java 1.2 interpreter and compiler, plus a text editor,
    were the stated requirements. The project later gained an Ant task and an
    Eclipse plugin called CodeGenClipse. Release 0.4.1 also added JavaScript
    templates through Rhino.
  </p>

  <h2 id="timeline">Release timeline</h2>

  <dl class="timeline">
    <dt><time datetime="2000-08-21">21 August 2000</time></dt>
    <dd>Jostraca 0.1.0a, the first release recorded by the old site.</dd>

    <dt><time datetime="2002-01-14">14 January 2002</time></dt>
    <dd>Jostraca 0.2.0a launched, followed by 0.2.1a two weeks later.</dd>

    <dt><time datetime="2002-06-15">15 June 2002</time></dt>
    <dd>The project appeared at ECOOP 2002.</dd>

    <dt><time datetime="2002-08-04">4 August 2002</time></dt>
    <dd>Jostraca 0.3.1 was released; 0.3.2 and 0.3.3 followed in 2003.</dd>

    <dt><time datetime="2004-11-26">26 November 2004</time></dt>
    <dd>Jostraca 0.4.0 was released.</dd>

    <dt><time datetime="2006-05-31">31 May 2006</time></dt>
    <dd>
      Jostraca 0.4.1 became the final release listed on the site. Binary and
      source distributions were published as ZIP and tar.gz archives.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="licence">Freedom to generate</h2>

  <p>
    The Java generator was distributed under version 2 of the GNU General Public
    License. Its site made a separate promise about output: code produced with
    Jostraca belonged entirely to the person who generated it. The generator did
    not impose an IDE, technology or layout on that output.
  </p>

  <h2 id="people">People</h2>

  <p>
    Richard Rodger maintained the project. The old credits name Morten
    Christensen for the Eclipse plugin, Orla Shanaghy as documentation editor,
    Derek Conniffe for hardware and server work, Lars Grammel for the Ant task,
    Tim Buchalka for <code>build.xml</code>, and Chris Perdue for
    <code>CommandRunner</code>. They also thank Eoin Shanaghy, Godfrey Nolan,
    Gerry Feeney, Alaister Moull, Conor Wyse and Caroline Hughes as reviewers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="today">What carried forward</h2>

  <p>
    The current Jostraca is a new TypeScript and Go implementation, not the Java
    0.4.x distribution. The runtime, component model and release line are new.
    What carried forward is the purpose: remove repetitive work by generating
    code from a model, while leaving the generated code useful and owned by the
    developer. The name and the ostracon mark carry that history visibly into
    the new project.
  </p>

  <p class="footnote">
    Read the
    <a href={ARCHIVE} rel="noopener">archived original site</a>, including its
    manuals, examples, release notes and surviving downloads. It is historical
    material; use the <a href="/docs">current documentation</a> for this project.
  </p>
</Prose>

<style>
  .legacy-mark {
    float: right;
    width: min(210px, 42%);
    margin: 8px 0 28px 36px;
  }
  .legacy-mark img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    box-shadow: 7px 8px 0 var(--secondary-soft);
  }
  .legacy-mark figcaption {
    margin-top: 14px;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-family: var(--font-head);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }
  .timeline dt {
    color: var(--secondary);
  }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .legacy-mark {
      float: none;
      width: min(210px, 70%);
      margin: 12px auto 40px;
    }
  }
</style>
